<Base lang="en" title="eDistrict Print: How to Print, Download and Verify Your Certificate" description="How to print, download and verify an eDistrict UP certificate — download the signed PDF from Completed Applications, check status with your application number, verify with Certificate Verification and QR, and fix common problems." path="/en/certificates-general/" altPath="/certificates-general/" faqs={faqs} updated="11 November 2026" datePublished="2026-11-11" dateModified="2026-11-11" howto={howto}>
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">Certificate Print, Download and Verification (eDistrict Print)</h1><p class="note">Note: This is an independent information guide — it is not the official portal (edistrict.up.gov.in).</p><p class="updated-line">The right route for printing, downloading and verifying</p>
<div class="action-grid"><a class="action-card" href="https://edistrict.up.gov.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Official eDistrict portal ↗</strong><span>Open print, download or verification</span></a><a class="action-card" href="https://esathi.up.gov.in/citizenservices/login/login.aspx" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Official citizen login ↗</strong><span>Print or download an issued document</span></a></div>
<section class="box"><h2>Decide what you need first</h2><p>There are three different certificate tasks on eDistrict, and each has its own route. Most confusion comes from picking the wrong one — so identify your task in the table below, then follow only its steps.</p><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Keep ready</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Print the certificate</td><td>Citizen Login; an issued certificate</td><td>Completed Applications → Print</td></tr><tr><td>Download the PDF</td><td>Citizen Login; status showing Certificate Generated</td><td>Completed Applications → Save as PDF</td></tr><tr><td>Verify a certificate</td><td>The Certificate Number printed on the certificate</td><td>Homepage → Certificate Verification</td></tr><tr><td>Track the application</td><td>Application/SRN number</td><td>Application Status (no login)</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">The application number (SRN) and the Certificate Number are different numbers. The SRN is issued when you apply and is only for tracking; the Certificate Number is printed on the final certificate and is what verification uses. Entering the SRN on the verification page returns no result.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>How to print a certificate</h2><p>Printing means taking a paper copy of an issued certificate. The printout comes from the same document you download — so first make sure your certificate has actually been issued.</p><ol><li>Log in to eSathi — open your dashboard at esathi.up.gov.in with your login ID and password.</li><li>On the dashboard, choose the Completed Applications section. Applications whose certificates have been issued appear in this list.</li><li>Click your application in the list — its full details open, including the option to view the certificate.</li><li>Click the Print button in the open application. The browser print dialog opens.</li><li>In the dialog, pick your printer — or, if you want a file, choose Save as PDF instead of a printer and save it.</li><li>Before printing, check the name spelling, date and address — if you spot an error after printing, do not edit the PDF; read the common problems section below.</li></ol><p>You receive a digitally signed PDF carrying the Certificate Number, a QR code and the Digital Signature (DSC) of the Tehsildar/SDM. That is the final, official certificate — there is no separate physical version of it. You can print as many copies as you like; every copy stays verifiable through its QR code and Certificate Number. The digital certificate is legally valid under Section 5 of the Information Technology Act, 2000 — courts, universities, government offices and banks all accept it.</p><div class="note">Prefer the portal's own Print/download button over the browser print — the portal button delivers the correctly formatted signed document.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>How to download a certificate</h2><p>There is one precondition for download: the status must show <strong>Certificate Generated</strong>. If it still shows Approved, wait for the signed PDF to be generated — download only works once the certificate has been issued.</p><ol><li>First check the status with your application number — enter the SRN under Application Status and see whether the certificate has been generated. This needs no login.</li><li>Log in to eSathi, open the dashboard and choose Completed Applications.</li><li>Click your application in the list to open its full details.</li><li>Press the Print button — in the print dialog, choose <strong>Save as PDF</strong> instead of a printer and save the file. That PDF is your official certificate.</li></ol><h3>Download from DigiLocker</h3><p>Log in at digilocker.gov.in and look for your certificate under Issued Documents. A DigiLocker document is equally valid and can be shared directly with an institution. Note — rely on a DigiLocker document only when it appears from the official issuer in Issued Documents.</p><h3>If you applied through a CSC</h3><p>The route is the same for CSC applicants: create your free eSathi account and download as above, or use DigiLocker, or collect a copy from the same centre. Having your own account makes every future task easier.</p><div class="note"><strong>Fees and validity:</strong> no fee amount is stated on this page — rely on the amount shown on the portal when you apply. Fee and validity details for each certificate are given in its own guide; see the related guides below.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>How to verify an issued certificate</h2><p>Verification is usually needed by a third party — a university checks a caste or domicile certificate at admission, a recruitment board checks income and domicile for a job, a bank verifies a death certificate for an insurance claim, and a court checks a certificate produced as evidence. The good news is that verification needs no login — anyone, from anywhere, can check any certificate.</p><h3>Method 1 — by Certificate Number</h3><ol><li>Open the homepage of the official portal, edistrict.up.gov.in.</li><li>Choose Certificate Verification on the homepage — this is a different page from application status.</li><li>Enter the Certificate Number — the number printed on the issued certificate. The SRN does not work here.</li><li>Fill in the captcha and search. The portal shows the applicant name, certificate type, issue date and issuing authority. Match these with the details printed on the certificate — agreement between the two confirms authenticity.</li></ol><h3>Method 2 — by QR code (fastest)</h3><p>Scan the QR code printed on the certificate with your phone camera and tap the link that opens. The verification page opens directly and the record appears. This is the quickest check when submitting a certificate at an institution — on opening the QR link, confirm that a government domain opens.</p><h3>What third-party verification means</h3><p>When an institution asks for your certificate, it checks its authenticity through this same verification — it does not need your login ID or password. So if anyone asks for your login, OTP or password in the name of verification, do not share it; verification always happens without login, using the Certificate Number or QR code.</p><div class="warn">No record on its own is not proof of fraud — a wrong identifier, an older migrated record or a temporary portal issue can also cause it. For a mismatch or no record, contact the issuing tehsil/registrar office.</div><p>If a PDF reader shows “signature invalid”, do not panic: most PDF readers do not recognise Indian government Certifying Authorities, so they show a warning. Open the certificate in Adobe Acrobat Reader, click the signature and choose Validate Signature — if it shows Valid, the certificate is genuine.</p><p>For birth/death records, the national <a href="https://dc.crsorgi.gov.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Civil Registration System</a> lists Uttar Pradesh among supported states.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Common problems and fixes</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cannot find the download button</td><td>Check the status first — it must show Certificate Generated. Then log in and open Completed Applications; the download control is there.</td></tr><tr><td>PDF appears blank</td><td>Delete the file and download again on a stable internet connection.</td></tr><tr><td>Portal does not open, or a server error appears</td><td>Use the Chrome browser, clear the cache, turn off the ad-blocker and try an incognito window. On mobile, switch on Desktop site mode.</td></tr><tr><td>Session keeps expiring</td><td>The portal ends the session after a period of inactivity — keep all details ready before you start a form or a print.</td></tr><tr><td>The certificate has an error in it</td><td>Do not think of editing the PDF — that breaks the digital signature. A fresh application with the correct details is the right route.</td></tr><tr><td>Verification returns “No record”</td><td>First check that you entered the Certificate Number (not the SRN) and that there is no typing mistake. Then try the QR method; if it still fails, contact the issuing tehsil/registrar.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If the problem continues, contact the helpdesk — official district pages publish the helpline <strong>0522-2304706</strong> and email <strong>ceghelpdesk@gmail.com</strong>. Never share your OTP or password with anyone.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Rules for keeping your certificate safe</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>Never edit the original PDF — editing breaks the digital signature and can make the certificate invalid.</li><li>Keep a copy of the original file in protected cloud storage and in an offline backup.</li><li>Rely on a DigiLocker document only when it appears from the official issuer in Issued Documents.</li><li>Read the receiving institution's issue-date and format conditions before submitting.</li><li>Never post your application number, Certificate Number or a copy of the PDF in a public group — save results privately.</li><li>You may keep several printed copies, but the original PDF is the real document — if a copy is lost, print again from the original file.</li></ul></section>
